nvk: no CPU writes into driver-owned video memory through BAR1 (tables, pools); system-memory host-visible type first.

RM's BAR1 mapping-reuse cache (open-gpu-kernel-modules src/nvidia/src/kernel/gpu/mem_mgr/mapping_reuse.c)
keys mappings by memory-descriptor pointer, and _kbusDestroyMemdescBar1Cb leaves the reuse entry, its
BAR1 VA and PTEs behind when a memory descriptor is destroyed while BAR1-mapped. A later BAR1 mapping
of another allocation that reuses that descriptor address hits the stale entry and the CPU reads and
writes somebody else's pages: copies and clears come back as zeros or stale values, texture fetches
through zero headers fault. Any process on the GPU can leave such entries, the stock desktop clients
included. The stock driver is not exposed: it never writes driver-owned video memory with the CPU.

Follow the stock driver's layout (libnvidia-eglcore 615.71.09):
* the system-memory HOST_VISIBLE|HOST_COHERENT|HOST_CACHED type precedes the BAR type, as stock
  lists its system-memory host-visible types before BAR VRAM;
* texture/sampler header tables stay in video memory without a CPU mapping and are written through
  the upload queue, whose copies every later queue submission waits for (nvk_queue.c); stock fills
  its header pools with copy-engine copies from a system-memory staging ring at the next submission
  (staged upload at ELF 0xa83c90) and its 3D/compute channels acquire them before the command buffer
  (shader code already goes this way, nvk-shader-upload-publish.patch);
* freed header slots are not cleared (stock writes nothing on vkDestroyImageView);
* descriptor pools, events and indirect execution sets are system memory.

Command/upload memory is handled by nvk-gpu-written-command-memory.patch (GPU-written with
inline-to-memory from the pushbuffer, as the stock driver writes its per-dispatch data).

